---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';
import { QUIZ_DURATION } from '../../lib/quiz';

const guide = getGuide('scholarships-for-medicine-hat-students');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    If you go to school in Medicine Hat, you have a scholarship advantage most Alberta students don't: a small applicant pool. A community award here might see a dozen applications in a good year, and the committees reading them know your school, your employers, and probably your referee. This guide maps every local pool worth checking, roughly twenty listings in <a href="/scholarships/">the directory</a> at last count.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with the money that isn't a competition</h2>
  <p>
    Before any local award, take the <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Alexander Rutherford Scholarship</a>. It pays up to $2,500 for the marks you already earned, every qualifying Alberta student who applies gets it, and it stacks with everything below. It is the floor, not the prize.
  </p>

  <h2>The local pools, in rough order of odds</h2>
  <h3>Your own school</h3>
  <p>
    Several awards never leave the building: the Hat High (MHHS) Alumni Memorial Scholarship, the Crescent Heights Leadership Award, Monsignor McCoy's Faith in Action Award. These are distributed through your guidance office, often with single-digit applicant counts. Ask for the awards list by name; some are only advertised on a sheet by the counsellor's door.
  </p>
  <h3>Service clubs</h3>
  <p>
    Medicine Hat's Rotary, Lions, Kinsmen, and Kin Canada branches all run student awards, typically $500 to $1,000. The applications are short, and the committees put real weight on part-time jobs, volunteering, and being a functional human — not on national-level achievement.
  </p>
  <h3>City, county, and community foundations</h3>
  <p>
    The City of Medicine Hat runs an environmental bursary, Cypress County has an agricultural scholarship, and the Community Foundation of Southeastern Alberta administers a stack of small named funds through one application. If your family farms or you live outside city limits, the county pool is especially thin on applicants.
  </p>
  <h3>Employers, co-ops, and local business</h3>
  <p>
    South Country Co-op runs a scholarship open to students as early as Grade 10, and awards also come from local real estate offices, the Exhibition &amp; Stampede, the police association, and the firefighters' charitable foundation. If you or a parent works somewhere with more than a few dozen employees, ask whether there's a staff-family award — there often is, and nobody applies.
  </p>
  <h3>Medicine Hat College</h3>
  <p>
    If you're staying local for school, MHC entrance scholarships reward the same marks Rutherford does — one more cheque for grades you already have.
  </p>

  <h2>How to actually work the list</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Filter the directory to Medicine Hat.</strong> The region filter in <a href="/scholarships/">the listings</a> shows the whole local pool at once, with live deadline status — most local awards run on a spring cycle and reopen every year.</li>
    <li><strong>Build one reusable package.</strong> One strong <a href="/guides/how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay/">essay</a> and one <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference letter</a> cover most local applications with light edits.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply to everything you plausibly qualify for.</strong> The <a href="/guides/local-scholarships-better-odds/">arithmetic on local awards</a> is heavily in your favour, and three $1,000 wins is a semester's tuition.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="/match/">The match quiz</a> takes {QUIZ_DURATION} and sorts the whole local list by what you're actually eligible for.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
